From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Youwes09 <yuvi.shen@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 00:11:05 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: Limit G16 performance requests to the top state

Region C 0x88 and 0x8c are the thermal and power performance-state
limits, and 0x98 caps the performance state the firmware picks, which it
rereads as it runs. All three are in hundredths of a state. They were
1000, one past the J713's top state 9; macOS 26.6.2 sets 900. Use the top
state from the power table, as the power controllers already do.

The meaning of 0x88 and 0x8c is from interface notes by Syd Garlathy
<s@ll.dev>, and that of 0x98 from interface notes by Ibrahim Syed
<ibrahim.syed787b@gmail.com>, both written from macOS's drivers. On the
J713, lowering 0x98 to 300 at run time drops effect2d from 5460 to 3260
FPS, and raising it back to 900 restores it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Youwes09 <yuvi.shen@outlook.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16_platform.rs | 8 +++++---
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16_platform.rs b/drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16_platform.rs
index 656073d455b9..dea9d8065f6d 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16_platform.rs
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/asahi/g16_platform.rs
@@ -375,6 +375,8 @@ pub(crate) fn region_c(&self) -> Result<KVec<u8>> {
         // Keep GFX clock-gated briefly before powering it off. On T8132,
         // 40 permits ACTUAL 4 between frames and state 0 after quiet time.
         put32(&mut out, REGION_C_CLOCK_GATED_OFF_DELAY_MS, 40);
+        // Performance-state limits, in hundredths of a state.
+        let top_state = 100 * self.max_pstate();
         for (off, value) in [
             (0x78, 1),
             (0x7ec, 1),
@@ -390,9 +392,9 @@ pub(crate) fn region_c(&self) -> Result<KVec<u8>> {
             (0x9cc, 64),
             (0x9d0, 2),
             (0x84, self.maximum_power),
-            (0x88, 1000),
-            (0x8c, 1000),
-            (0x98, 1000),
+            (0x88, top_state),
+            (0x8c, top_state),
+            (0x98, top_state),
         ] {
             put32(&mut out, off, value);
         }
